---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About" description="What the Open Health Index lists, how tools get in, and what a listing does and doesn't mean.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      The Open Health Index lists free and open-source tools for people looking after their own health: tracking
      symptoms and flares, mood, medications and cycles, and keeping their own records. It's for patients,
      caregivers and anyone who wants their health data to stay theirs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Software for clinics, hospitals and health IT is out of scope. If that's what you need, try
      <a href="https://github.com/kakoni/awesome-healthcare">awesome-healthcare</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What gets listed</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The source code is public under an OSI-approved open-source license.</li>
      <li>The tool is useful to an individual, not only to a clinic or developer.</li>
      <li>It has had a release or code change in the last 18 months, or is marked as watchlist or no longer maintained.</li>
      <li>The parts you need to actually use it are open. Tools that are open source in name but lock the usable product behind a proprietary license aren't listed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What a listing means</h2>
    <p>
      A listing means a tool meets the criteria above. It is not a medical recommendation, and it doesn't mean the
      tool is safe or right for you. Some tools, especially the community-built insulin systems, can affect your
      treatment. Talk to your care team before changing how you manage your health.
    </p>
    <p>
      Facts we haven't confirmed are shown as <em>Not yet checked</em> rather than guessed.
    </p>

    <h2>Disclosure</h2>
    <p>
      This index is run by the people behind <a href="https://github.com/Health-Flare">Health-Flare</a>, who also make
      health tracking apps. Our own tools are marked on their listings.
    </p>

    <h2>Contribute</h2>
    <p>
      Every listing is a small file in the
      <a href="https://github.com/Health-Flare/open-health-index">GitHub repository</a>. You can suggest a tool or fix
      a listing there without writing code.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 42rem; }
  .prose h1 { margin-top: 0; font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.4rem); }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 32px; font-size: 1.3rem; }
  .prose a { color: var(--link); }
</style>
